0,Meriam language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meriam_language,"May 18, 2025 - In 2005 Malcolm Ross concluded that the Eastern Trans-Fly languages were not part of the Trans–New Guinea phylum. R. M. W. Dixon (2002) regards claims of a relationship between the Fly River languages and Meriam as unproven, though what he bases his claim on is not clear, as Meriam Mir has a ...",Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
1,Meriam people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meriam_people,"February 10, 2026 - The stories told by elders to the ... for the transgenerational learning of the Meriam culture. Some of the stories explain why certain rituals or practices are important in Indigenous clans. Torres Strait Islanders in particular the Mer people are seafarers, sea country was used as a place for gardening, hunting, fishing, ceremonies, art, music, dance and ...",Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
2,Torres Strait Island languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torres_Strait_Island_languages,"March 10, 2026 - It belongs to the Pama–Nyungan languages of the Australian mainland. The other indigenous language spoken mainly in the eastern islands is Meriam Mir: a member of the Trans-Fly languages spoken on the nearby south coast of New Guinea and the only Papuan language spoken on Australian territory.",Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
3,Eastern Trans-Fly languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eastern_Trans-Fly_languages,"December 30, 2025 - The following basic vocabulary words for Bine (Täti dialect), Bine (Sogal dialect), Gizra (Kupere dialect) and Wipi (Dorogori dialect) are from the Trans-New Guinea database. The equivalent words for Meriam Mir are also included.",Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
4,Category:Eastern Trans-Fly languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Eastern_Trans-Fly_languages,Eastern Trans-Fly languages · Bine language · Gizrra language · Meriam language · Wipi language · Categories: Culture of the Torres Strait Islands · Trans-Fly–Bulaka River languages · Languages of Papua New Guinea · Trans-Fly languages · Search · Category:Eastern Trans-Fly languages ·,Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
5,"Meriam Mir language, alphabet and pronunciation - Omniglot",https://www.omniglot.com/writing/meriammir.htm,"Apr 23, 2021 · Meriam Mir is spoken by about 2,000 people on the Torres Strait Islands of Erub, Ugar and Mer between Papua New Guinea and the mainland of Australian. Some linguists classify Miriam Mir as a member of the Eastern Trans-Fly language family of Trans-New Guinea.",Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
6,Revitalizing Meriam Mir Through Song | PDF | Hymns - Scribd,https://www.scribd.com/document/827733567/Revising-Meriam-Mir-Through-Song,"The document discusses the efforts of the eastern Torres Strait Islander community to preserve and revitalize the endangered Meriam Mir language through sacred songs, particularly Christian hymns.",Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
7,Meriam Mir / Miriam Mer Torres Strait Traditional Language ...,https://www.tstlanguages.org/our-languages/meriam-mir,Meriam Mir is one of two traditional languages of the Torres Strait. Meriam Mir (also written as Miriam Mer) is the language of the Eastern Islands of the Torres Strait.,Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
8,MERIAM MER LANGUAGE - OUR FAMILY,https://hodgsonlowah.weebly.com/meriam-mer-language.html,"Bomai: secret and sacred name of the god of the Meriam (see also Malo). dabor: mackerel. daip: mound made of shell and vegetable matter forming boundary between allotments. Dauar: one of the three Murray Islands. deumer: Torres Strait pigeon. elikup: boundary marker. ged: home-place, homeland, habitable island with water resources, womb.",Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
9,Meriam language - Wikiwand,https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Meriam+Mir,"A group of Torres Strait Islander people in Mackay region, where there are only four fluent speakers left, are practising and teaching traditional hymns sung in Meriam Mir in an effort to help more people to learn the language and pass it down.",Meriam Mir,Eastern Trans-Fly
